An array is a foundational data structure that stores a collection of elements of the same size and data type in contiguous memory locations. 
Each element is identified by an index, allowing for instant, direct access to any item.

Core Characteristics of Array:-
Homogeneous: Usually holds elements of the identical data type (e.g., all integers or all strings).
Contiguous Memory: Elements are stored right next to each other in memory, making them highly efficient.
Indexed: Positions are assigned numbers, usually starting at 0 (zero-based indexing).
Fixed Size: In many languages like Java or C, the size must be declared upfront and cannot be dynamically changed.

Common Operations:-
Traversal: Visiting every element in the array one by one.
Insertion/Deletion: Adding or removing an element (which can be slow if shifting all subsequent elements is required).
Search: Finding the location of a specific element.
Accessing: Looking up a specific value using its exact index. 

Multidimensional Arrays - Arrays can have more than one dimension, functioning essentially as "arrays of arrays".
1D Array: A single list or sequence of items.
2D Array: A grid structured with rows and columns, used to represent matrices or tables.
3D Array: A collection of two-dimensional arrays. It can be visualised as multiple 2D arrays stacked on top of each other.
